  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Near normal temperatures and isolated to widely \nscattered afternoon/evening showers and storms are expected to \ncontinue through the weekend. \n\nHigh pressure will remain anchored off the Southeast coast through \nthe weekend. The upper ridge offshore will build westward today with \nmodest height rises keeping a lid on widespread convection. 00z \nmodels and CAM guidance show the potential for relatively greater \nshower and storm chances across northeastern NC early this afternoon \nbut PoPs remain below 40%. Another area of showers and storms will \nlikely form west and northwest of the local area along the higher \nBlue Ridge terrain by mid to late afternoon. These storms may just \nbe able to sneak into the far NW and N portions of the area by the \nevening but most of the region will be dry.  \n\nWith lower storm chances, temps rebound to the upper 80s-lower 90s \non today with upper 90s-low 100s heat indices. Similar conditions \nare expected Friday into the weekend with some slight height falls \nover the local area as the upper ridge offshore retreats a bit to \nthe SE and a series of weak shortwaves pass by to our N/NW. This \nwill lead to slightly greater chances for diurnally driven showers \nor storms on Friday and Saturday across western and northern \nportions of the area but PoPs generally remain aob 40%. A few storms \nare possible on Sunday, with the highest precip chances (still only \n30-40%) shifting to SE VA/NE NC. Seasonable heat and humidity will \ncontinue through the weekend, with heat indices in the upper 90s-low \n100s each day.",
